<h2>1. Explain the purpose</h2>
<p>Begin with what the model needs to support.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<ul>
<li>Internal design evaluation</li>
<li>Planning or government approval</li>
<li>Stakeholder or community presentation</li>
<li>Investor or board meeting</li>
<li>Sales gallery or marketing environment</li>
<li>Construction coordination</li>
<li>Exhibition or permanent display</li>
<li>As-built documentation</li>
</ul>
<p>The intended use helps determine what should be emphasized and what can be simplified.</p>

<h2>2. Identify the audience</h2>
<p>Explain who needs to understand the model.</p>
<p>An audience of architects and consultants may need different information from investors, approval agencies, buyers, community members, or the general public.</p>

<h2>3. Define what should be included</h2>
<p>Describe the desired model boundary:</p>
<ul>
<li>Building only</li>
<li>Building and landscape</li>
<li>Adjacent buildings and streets</li>
<li>Full development site</li>
<li>Interior spaces</li>
<li>Section through the building</li>
<li>Regional or urban context</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are uncertain, provide the site information and explain what relationships the audience needs to understand.</p>

<h2>4. Provide approximate dimensions</h2>
<p>Include any known dimensions, such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Building height</li>
<li>Building footprint</li>
<li>Site dimensions</li>
<li>Desired model size</li>
<li>Available display area</li>
</ul>
<p>Exact dimensions are not required for an initial review. Approximate measurements are enough to begin comparing possible scales.</p>

<h2>5. Describe the required detail</h2>
<p>Identify features that are important to the model's purpose:</p>
<ul>
<li>Massing and overall form</li>
<li>Façades and glazing</li>
<li>Structural relationships</li>
<li>Entrances and circulation</li>
<li>Landscape and topography</li>
<li>Roads, vehicles and people</li>
<li>Interiors or sectional relationships</li>
<li>Lighting</li>
<li>Removable or interchangeable components</li>
</ul>
<p>It is also helpful to identify areas that can remain simplified.</p>

<h2>6. Share the available information</h2>
<p>Send whatever is currently available:</p>
<ul>
<li>CAD, BIM or 3D files</li>
<li>PDFs and dimensioned drawings</li>
<li>Site plans and elevations</li>
<li>Renderings and photographs</li>
<li>Material references</li>
<li>Sketches or concept drawings</li>
<li><a href="/blog/from-ai-generated-image-to-physical-model">AI-generated reference images</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Complete files are not required for the initial review. We will identify any missing information or separately scoped Digital Model Development needed before fabrication.</p>

<h2>7. Confirm the schedule and location</h2>
<p>Provide:</p>
<ul>
<li>Required completion date</li>
<li>Date of any fixed presentation or event</li>
<li>Delivery address</li>
<li>Final display location</li>
<li>Building-access information</li>
<li>Installation or setup requirements</li>
</ul>
<p>These details can affect scale, sectional construction, <a href="/blog/architectural-model-packaging-shipping-installation">packaging, transportation</a>, and the production schedule.</p>

<h2>Architectural model brief checklist</h2>
<p>A concise initial brief can include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Project name and location</li>
<li>Intended use</li>
<li>Audience</li>
<li>Model boundary</li>
<li>Approximate dimensions or preferred scale</li>
<li>Required details and features</li>
<li>Available files</li>
<li>Desired completion date</li>
<li>Delivery and installation location</li>
<li>Primary contact and decision-makers</li>
</ul>
